Rasterize the audience view off the UI thread

Purpose: scroll the main canvas smoothly while the presentation window
is open. GTK draws every window on one thread, and the audience view
rasterized each page it reached there: with Manim slides the main
canvas stalled on every page change.

Approach:
- The audience raster cache works in the background: before a draw the
  engine orders the rasters of the page, one worker thread makes them,
  and they come back as EngineTask::AudienceRaster. The zoom window's
  cache still rasterizes while drawing.
- The audience stays on the page it shows until the rasters of the
  wanted page are there (Presentation::settle_on), so it never draws a
  page without them.
- The pages above and below are ordered ahead, like the margin around
  the main canvas' viewport. They are placed on the page grid: a raster
  fits only the exact page.
- Rasters of pages left are dropped as before; their orders are called
  off.
- A slide's take is converted by the job that draws its still frame,
  and the player keeps the result for playing. LiveStrokes::still_job
  hands that job out.
- One worker, not the rayon pool: the pool stays with the main canvas,
  and slides cache their pictures per thread.
- A raster that fails or panics is not ordered again; its stroke is
  drawn immediate, so no view waits forever.

Constraints: playing slides are still drawn on the UI thread each
frame. Stepping a slide before its still frame is ready converts it on
the UI thread. No change to the file format or to stock behaviour
without a presentation window.
